                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "LOST CROWN: Ghost Hunt – Review\r\nI've genuinely tried to enjoy this game and give it a fair chance, but man, it is poorly made.\r\n\r\nFirst off, the pacing is absolutely terrible. Everything feels painfully slow. It takes forever just to move your character around, and don't expect him to run because apparently that's asking too much. If you're lucky, you might be able to move through certain areas a little faster by double-clicking the arrows, but even that feels clunky.\r\n\r\nThe black-and-white visual style is another issue. While this kind of art direction can work well for certain games, I really don't think it works here. Instead of adding atmosphere, it strips away any personality or visual flavour the game might have had. Everything just looks dull, bland, and honestly rather boring.\r\n\r\nThen there's the voice acting and characters. Almost everyone, including the main protagonist, seems completely tone-deaf to what's happening around them. There's very little emotional reaction to anything, and the dialogue is delivered in such a monotone way that it's difficult to feel invested in the characters or what's going on. The protagonist himself just doesn't seem to fit. Whether it's the character model, the animations, or something else, something about him feels completely out of place.\r\n\r\nI will say that I think the story had potential. There's the foundation of something that could have been a genuinely interesting game here, but it feels like nobody really put in the extra effort needed to bring it all together. It's frustrating because you can see glimpses of what this game could have been, but the execution just isn't there.\r\n\r\nThe sound effects are actually pretty good for this type of game, and the music does a decent job of creating an atmospheric ambience. Unfortunately, that's about where my compliments end. What's the point of having good atmosphere and sound design when so many other aspects of the game drag the experience down?\r\n\r\nThe clues and puzzles are another major problem. Some of them are so obscure that I honestly don't think most players would figure them out without looking up a guide. There were several moments where I had to resort to one, only to find myself thinking, \"But I already did that!\" Some triggers seem incredibly inconsistent, while others are so easy to miss that you can end up wandering around wondering what you're supposed to do next. A little more clarity and better feedback would have gone a long way here.\r\n\r\nOverall, I feel like LOST CROWN: Ghost Hunt could have been a much better game than what we ended up getting. It has some decent ideas, a potentially interesting story, and some good atmospheric sound design, but the poor pacing, lifeless characters, questionable presentation, and frustrating clue progression make it difficult to enjoy.\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, I just can't recommend this one. I wanted to like it, I really did, but in the end, it simply wasn't worth the time.",
